// The clean (no-struct) repro of the enum-match permission cycle, from the
// Prusti translation of:
//
//     enum Case { One, Two, Three, Four }
//     fn add(v: &mut i32, case: Case) {
//         match case {
//             Case::One => *v = *v + 1, Case::Two => *v = *v + 2,
//             Case::Three => *v = *v + 3, Case::Four => *v = *v + 4,
//         }
//     }
//
// `&mut i32` is a reference cell whose pointee is held generically (`p_Param`);
// each arm converts to `p_Int_i32` and back, scaled by its branch flag. Because
// VMIR linearizes the CFG, all (mutually-exclusive) arms thread through one
// heap, so at the exit `m_add#ensures` exhale the held permission is a *nested
// indicator partition* `ite(discr==0, 1, ite(discr==1, 1, … 0))`-shaped sum,
// one summand per arm. Its top node is `+` (which `lt-ite` cannot descend), and
// a tier-4 case split needs depth N — so the failure appeared at the 4th arm
// (1-3 arms happened to fit the split budget; the 4th blew it).
//
// Fixed by `merge_ite_sum` (context.rs), reached from the `prove_perm_ineq`
// failure path in `heap_subtract`: merge same-condition summands
// (`ite(c,a,x)+ite(c,b,y) => ite(c, a+b, x+y)`), collapsing the whole partition
// to `ite(∨ discr==k, 1, 0)` in O(N) steps — no case split, scales to any arm
// count (verified by hand out to 10 arms; not in the corpus as the translated
// file is large). Companion of `point_step_perm_cycle.vpr` (same shape behind a
// two-field struct).
